          {
            "title": "Concept",
            "paragraphs": [
              "A state machine enumerates allowed states and transitions. A run can be ready, waiting for approval, uncertain, done, denied, cancelled or limited. Each describes different behavior. A waiting run is not successful; a timed-out operation may be uncertain rather than failed. A terminal state prevents accidental execution after completion or cancellation."
            ]
          },
          {
            "title": "Example",
            "paragraphs": [
              "Write transitions before coding an unconstrained while loop. The workshop uses these state names in its observable result."
            ],
            "example": "ready → valid read → ready\nready → valid write proposal → waiting\nwaiting → matching approval → ready / execute\nwaiting → denial → denied\nready → response lost after effect → uncertain\nuncertain → trusted receipt reconciliation → ready\nready → final → done\nnonterminal → cancellation / budget stop → terminal"
          },
          {
            "title": "Tradeoffs",
            "paragraphs": [
              "State and event are different. \"Tool started\" is an event; \"waiting\" is a state. A broad exception handler that calls every problem failed erases whether an effect happened. Make illegal transitions visible and reject them; repeated calls to a terminal run should return the terminal result without new tool work."
            ]
          }
