        "id": "audit",
        "title": "20. Make telemetry useful without leaking content",
        "stage": "advanced",
        "takeaway": "Record enough metadata to diagnose decisions while minimizing sensitive payloads.",
        "sections": [
          {
            "title": "Concept",
            "paragraphs": [
              "Operational events can include run ID, tool name, state transition, duration, policy version and outcome category. Full prompts, arguments and outputs can contain personal data, credentials or confidential notes. Prefer an allowlist of safe event fields and controlled references to restricted evidence. Redaction by a few keyword replacements is not a guarantee: secrets can appear in unexpected fields or encodings."
            ]
          },
          {
            "title": "Example",
            "paragraphs": [
              "The workshop events omit note text and raw exception strings. A separate trusted state may still contain sensitive drafts; minimized logs do not make all state non-sensitive."
            ],
            "example": "Event: tool_confirmed\nFields: run_id, call_id, tool, status, receipt_id\nOmitted: note text, lesson body, credentials\nMetric: count by tool/outcome (bounded labels)\nAvoid per-note text or arbitrary call IDs as metric labels."
          },
          {
            "title": "Tradeoffs",
            "paragraphs": [
              "Logs need access control, retention, deletion and integrity decisions. Even metadata can reveal activity patterns. Metrics, traces and audit evidence have different users and retention needs. Do not log hidden model reasoning as an audit requirement; observable decisions, tool proposals, policy verdicts and outcomes are the appropriate evidence for this workshop."
            ]
          }
        ],
