        "id": "grounding",
        "title": "9. Grounding answers in evidence",
        "stage": "foundation",
        "takeaway": "Grounding connects an answer to available evidence. A citation helps the reader inspect support; it does not automatically make a claim correct.",
        "sections": [
          {
            "title": "Concept",
            "paragraphs": [
              "Grounding connects an answer to available evidence. A citation helps the reader inspect support; it does not automatically make a claim correct.",
              "A lesson snippet states that an `INNER JOIN` keeps matching pairs. A grounded answer can explain that point and cite the lesson. It should not invent performance guarantees absent from the source. Separate a directly supported statement, an inference and an unknown. When evidence is missing, return a useful limitation or ask a focused question."
            ]
          },
          {
            "title": "Example",
            "paragraphs": [
              "The excerpt supports join semantics, while performance claims require different evidence. An existing citation ID alone cannot establish support."
            ],
            "example": "Evidence SQL-07: \"INNER JOIN returns matching row pairs.\"\nSupported: \"Unmatched rows are excluded by this join.\"\nNeeds more evidence: \"It is always faster than LEFT JOIN.\"\nUnknown: \"Your production query will finish in 12 ms.\"\nCitation check: ID exists\nSupport check: cited content actually supports the claim"
          },
          {
            "title": "Tradeoffs",
            "paragraphs": [
              "Retrieval can return outdated, incomplete or misleading text. Preserve source revision or date when it matters and compare conflicting evidence. An automatic citation-membership check cannot evaluate entailment. Human review or calibrated content checks remain necessary for important claims."
            ]
          }
        ],
