        "id": "retrieval",
        "title": "13. Retrieval: finding useful context",
        "stage": "intermediate",
        "takeaway": "Retrieval selects evidence from a collection before answering.",
        "sections": [
          {
            "title": "Concept",
            "paragraphs": [
              "Retrieval selects evidence from a collection before answering. Keyword search, semantic search and hybrid ranking are methods with different failure modes.",
              "Keyword matching may find SQL joins precisely but miss a paraphrase about combining rows. Embeddings represent content numerically for similarity search, but high similarity does not establish truth or permission. Chunking divides documents into retrievable units; chunks need enough surrounding context and stable source references to be useful."
            ]
          },
          {
            "title": "Example",
            "paragraphs": [
              "Reformulating the query can improve recall. Retrieval relevance and faithful answer generation remain separate evaluation questions."
            ],
            "example": "Question: \"How do I combine matching records?\"\nKeyword query: \"combine matching records\" -> possibly no hit\nReformulated query: \"INNER JOIN\" -> SQL-07\nEvidence package: ID + revision + excerpt + source location\nEvaluate separately: was the right evidence retrieved?\nThen evaluate: did the answer use it correctly?"
          },
          {
            "title": "Tradeoffs",
            "paragraphs": [
              "Access controls should filter the retrievable collection for the caller, not rely on the model to ignore forbidden results. Ranking quality can deteriorate when content changes. The workshop uses simple lexical matching on a synthetic public collection; it does not implement embeddings or prove retrieval quality on real documents."
            ]
          },
          {
            "title": "Diagnose retrieval before changing the prompt",
            "paragraphs": [
              "A research question asks how to recover a failed import. Document A describes setup; document B describes rollback; document C contains an old rollback policy. A fluent answer from A alone is a retrieval failure before it is a writing failure. Keep the selected IDs visible and distinguish missing evidence from contradictory evidence.",
              "Try three questions against the same small corpus: one answerable verbatim, one paraphrase, and one absent topic. A lexical baseline may miss the paraphrase; returning any nonempty list must not automatically permit an answer. First inspect selection, then generation, then whether each claim is supported. An embedding model can change ranking without solving document freshness or access control.",
              "In `local_framework_lab.py`, the fixed research records let you isolate routing and model behaviour. Replace one record only after the deterministic tests pass. Retain the no-evidence case while extending retrieval; otherwise every question can appear answerable because the retriever always returns something."
            ]
          }
        ],
