        "id": "failures",
        "title": "18. Failures, retries and uncertain effects",
        "stage": "intermediate",
        "takeaway": "Classify failures before retrying.",
        "sections": [
          {
            "title": "Concept",
            "paragraphs": [
              "Classify failures before retrying. Invalid arguments, permission denial, temporary unavailability and unknown execution outcomes need different responses.",
              "A malformed query should be repaired or rejected, not retried unchanged. A temporary read timeout may justify a bounded retry with backoff. A write timeout can mean the write happened but the response was lost. Retrying that write requires an idempotency contract or reconciliation, not optimism. Retry attempts consume time and budget."
            ]
          },
          {
            "title": "Example",
            "paragraphs": [
              "The table separates repairable input, denied authority, transient availability, uncertain writes and missing evidence."
            ],
            "example": "Failure                 Next action\nInvalid query           explain or repair within budget\nPermission denied       stop; do not bypass policy\nTemporary read failure  bounded retry if useful\nUnknown write outcome   reconcile by operation identity\nNo relevant evidence    narrow question or acknowledge gap"
          },
          {
            "title": "Tradeoffs",
            "paragraphs": [
              "A generic catch-all retry can amplify load and repeat external effects. Nested retries across model, tool adapter and client can multiply unexpectedly. Record attempts and use a total deadline. The workshop stops on tool failures to keep its behavior inspectable; it does not implement a live retry policy."
            ]
          }
        ],
