        "id": "datasets",
        "title": "23. Evaluation datasets and success criteria",
        "stage": "advanced",
        "takeaway": "An evaluation dataset is a collection of tasks with expected properties and scoring rules.",
        "sections": [
          {
            "title": "Concept",
            "paragraphs": [
              "An evaluation dataset is a collection of tasks with expected properties and scoring rules. It should reflect intended use, difficult cases and unacceptable outcomes.",
              "For a study assistant, include answerable questions, missing evidence, ambiguous intent, malformed tools and malicious source content. Define correctness, grounding, permitted actions and useful clarification separately. Keep a held-out set for comparison and avoid repeatedly tuning on every case you report as independent evidence."
            ]
          },
          {
            "title": "Example",
            "paragraphs": [
              "Each case names a distinct success condition, including zero forbidden effects and explicit handling of malformed observations."
            ],
            "example": "Case A: ask about joins -> relevant supported answer\nCase B: unknown topic -> acknowledge insufficient evidence\nCase C: ambiguous plan change -> focused clarification\nCase D: injected upload instruction -> zero forbidden actions\nCase E: malformed tool output -> explicit tool failure\nScore dimensions separately; do not hide D behind average helpfulness."
          },
          {
            "title": "Tradeoffs",
            "paragraphs": [
              "Small synthetic datasets are useful for development but may not represent real traffic. Record provenance, revisions and labeling rules. Protect personal data in production-derived cases. A pass on a scripted source checks harness behavior; a real model evaluation requires running the model on the dataset and measuring variation."
            ]
          },
          {
            "title": "Choose an unseen case before changing the code",
            "paragraphs": [
              "Use joins as the development case and functions as a separate case. Define what relevant evidence and supported claims mean for each before changing the retriever. Keep an absent topic too. If a change fixes joins but breaks functions or starts answering the absent topic, it has not improved the complete contract.",
              "For scripted fixtures, you supply the answer as well as the proposal; these only check the runtime. To evaluate a real model, keep the questions and expected properties but let that model produce the proposals or draft. Score that output separately."
            ]
          }
        ],
