        "id": "traces",
        "title": "24. Outcome checks and trace diagnosis",
        "stage": "advanced",
        "takeaway": "Outcome evaluation asks whether the task result was correct. Trace inspection explains how the system reached that result and where failures occurred.",
        "sections": [
          {
            "title": "Concept",
            "paragraphs": [
              "Outcome evaluation asks whether the task result was correct. Trace inspection explains how the system reached that result and where failures occurred.",
              "A correct answer can be produced after an unauthorized tool call, so output correctness alone is insufficient. A clean trace can also lead to an unsupported answer. Record proposal types, validation outcomes, tool names, result status, timings and version identifiers. Do not log every secret-bearing payload by default."
            ]
          },
          {
            "title": "Example",
            "paragraphs": [
              "The first trace points to source availability. The second confirms containment while leaving task completion to a separate outcome check."
            ],
            "example": "Trace: propose search -> accepted -> source unavailable -> failed\nOutcome: no answer delivered; truthful failure report\nDiagnosis: retrieval adapter availability, not answer formatting\n\nTrace: unknown upload -> denied -> final reassuring sentence\nOutcome: security boundary held; requested task may still be incomplete"
          },
          {
            "title": "Tradeoffs",
            "paragraphs": [
              "A trace is not hidden model reasoning and should not be presented as a complete explanation of internal thought. Generated rationales can be useful text but are not authoritative causal evidence. Prefer application events for operational diagnosis and apply redaction, access control and retention to logs."
            ]
          }
        ],
