        "id": "judges",
        "title": "25. Human and model judges need calibration",
        "stage": "advanced",
        "takeaway": "Some qualities need judgment rather than exact string matching.",
        "sections": [
          {
            "title": "Concept",
            "paragraphs": [
              "Some qualities need judgment rather than exact string matching. A judge needs a clear rubric, representative examples and comparison against trusted human labels.",
              "A groundedness rubric might ask whether every material claim is supported, whether uncertainty is stated and whether citations point to the correct source. Use deterministic checks for exact IDs or forbidden actions, then human or model review for semantic qualities. Keep a disagreement process; a model judge is another fallible system."
            ]
          },
          {
            "title": "Example",
            "paragraphs": [
              "The unsupported case is falsely accepted by the judge. That disagreement matters beyond the overall agreement count."
            ],
            "example": "Human labels: supported, unsupported, unclear\nJudge labels:  supported, supported, unclear\nDisagreement: case 2 needs review\nPossible causes: vague rubric, missed evidence, judge bias\nReport agreement AND important false-accept cases\nDo not report \"2/3 agreement\" as proof of safety."
          },
          {
            "title": "Tradeoffs",
            "paragraphs": [
              "Judges can prefer longer answers, their own style or a particular answer position. Blind version labels where practical, vary comparison order and review errors by category. Calibrate on held-out labeled cases and repeat important comparisons. A judge score is not a probability that an answer is true."
            ]
          }
        ],
