        "id": "ssrf",
        "title": "13. Restrict the requests your server makes for users",
        "stage": "intermediate",
        "takeaway": "When a user can influence a URL your server fetches, allowlist the destinations, check every resolved address, connect to the checked address, and limit outbound network access as a second layer.",
        "sections": [
          {
            "title": "How server-side request forgery works",
            "paragraphs": [
              "A feature such as \"import a note from a URL\" or \"fetch a link preview\" makes the server send a request the user chose. The server sits inside your network, so it can reach what the attacker cannot: admin ports on localhost, internal APIs, databases and the cloud instance metadata endpoint at 169.254.169.254, which can return credentials. The response, an error message or even the response time can leak what was reached.",
              "Blocklists of strings fail: addresses have many spellings (decimal, IPv6-mapped, short forms), redirects can point anywhere after the check, URL parsers disagree about `user@host` forms, and DNS can return a public address when you check and a private one when you connect (DNS rebinding). OWASP's Top 10:2025 places SSRF under Broken Access Control because the server is acting with authority the user does not have (lesson 4)."
            ]
          },
          {
            "title": "An allowlist with address checks",
            "paragraphs": [
              "Allow only the schemes, hosts and ports the feature needs. Resolve the name, reject any non-public address, then connect to that exact address so the check and the connection cannot diverge; disable redirects or re-check each hop; set short timeouts and a response size limit; and never return raw upstream errors. The stub resolver keeps the example offline. Notice that the `user@host` URL is parsed with the metadata address as its host and is rejected.",
              "Add a network layer: run fetchers in an egress-restricted subnet or through a proxy that enforces the allowlist, and require session tokens for instance metadata (for example IMDSv2 on AWS) so a simple GET cannot read credentials."
            ],
            "example": "import ipaddress\nfrom urllib.parse import urlsplit\n\nALLOWED_HOSTS = {'images.example.org'}            # destinations this feature needs\nDNS = {'images.example.org': ['93.184.215.14'],    # stub resolver: a public address\n       'evil.example': ['169.254.169.254']}\n\ndef check(url):\n    parts = urlsplit(url)\n    if parts.scheme != 'https':\n        return 'blocked: scheme'\n    host = parts.hostname or ''\n    if host not in ALLOWED_HOSTS:\n        return 'blocked: host not allowlisted'\n    for address in DNS.get(host, []):              # check every resolved address\n        ip = ipaddress.ip_address(address)\n        if not ip.is_global:\n            return f'blocked: {ip} is not a public address'\n    return f'allowed: connect to {DNS[host][0]} with Host {host}'\n\nfor url in ['https://images.example.org/cat.png', 'http://images.example.org/cat.png',\n            'https://evil.example/latest/meta-data/', 'https://127.0.0.1:8080/admin',\n            'https://images.example.org@169.254.169.254/']:\n    print(url, '->', check(url))\n\n# Expected output:\n# https://images.example.org/cat.png -> allowed: connect to 93.184.215.14 with Host images.example.org\n# http://images.example.org/cat.png -> blocked: scheme\n# https://evil.example/latest/meta-data/ -> blocked: host not allowlisted\n# https://127.0.0.1:8080/admin -> blocked: host not allowlisted\n# https://images.example.org@169.254.169.254/ -> blocked: host not allowlisted"
          }
        ],
