        "id": "vectorize-not-apply",
        "title": "10. Prefer vectorised operations to row-wise apply",
        "stage": "intermediate",
        "takeaway": "Whole-column operations run in compiled code; row-wise apply calls Python once per row. Use vectorised arithmetic, np.where and np.select, and keep apply for logic that cannot be expressed on columns.",
        "sections": [
          {
            "title": "Understand the decision",
            "paragraphs": [
              "`df.apply(f, axis=1)` builds a Series for each row and calls a Python function on it, so its cost grows with Python overhead per row. Column arithmetic, comparisons, `.str` and `.dt` methods operate on whole arrays in compiled code. For conditional values use `np.where` for two outcomes and `np.select` for several, with conditions checked in order and an explicit default.",
              "Vectorised code is not only faster; it states the rule once for the column, which makes boundary checks (lesson 9) easier to test. Keep apply (or a plain loop) for logic that genuinely needs per-row Python, such as calling a parser that has no vectorised form, and measure before optimising anything else."
            ]
          },
          {
            "title": "Worked example",
            "paragraphs": [
              "Both versions assign the same service level to 100,000 tickets. The row-wise version is many times slower; the exact ratio depends on the machine, so the example prints only whether the vectorised version was at least five times faster."
            ],
            "example": "import time\nimport numpy as np\nimport pandas as pd\n\nrng = np.random.default_rng(7)\ndf = pd.DataFrame({\"hours\": rng.integers(0, 100, 100_000),\n                   \"priority\": rng.choice([\"high\", \"low\"], 100_000)})\n\ndef level(row):\n    if row[\"priority\"] == \"high\" and row[\"hours\"] > 8:\n        return \"breach\"\n    if row[\"hours\"] > 48:\n        return \"breach\"\n    return \"ok\" if row[\"hours\"] <= 24 else \"late\"\n\nstart = time.perf_counter()\nby_row = df.apply(level, axis=1)\nrow_seconds = time.perf_counter() - start\n\nstart = time.perf_counter()\nbreach = ((df[\"priority\"] == \"high\") & (df[\"hours\"] > 8)) | (df[\"hours\"] > 48)\nby_column = pd.Series(np.select([breach, df[\"hours\"] <= 24], [\"breach\", \"ok\"], default=\"late\"))\ncolumn_seconds = time.perf_counter() - start\n\nprint(by_row.equals(by_column), by_column.value_counts().sort_index().to_dict())\nprint(\"vectorised at least 5x faster:\", row_seconds > 5 * column_seconds)\n\n# Output:\n# True {'breach': 71283, 'late': 11868, 'ok': 16849}\n# vectorised at least 5x faster: True"
          }
        ],
        "exercise": {
          "prompt": "A report labels each ticket `quick` (under 2 hours), `normal` (2 to 24 hours) or `slow` (over 24 hours), and missing durations must stay unlabelled. The current code uses apply with if statements and takes minutes on a large file. Rewrite the rule with vectorised operations and say how you would show the result is unchanged.",
          "solution": "Use np.select with conditions [hours < 2, hours <= 24, hours > 24] and choices ['quick', 'normal', 'slow'], with default set to a missing value so missing durations, which fail every comparison, stay unlabelled (or pd.cut with bins [0, 2, 24, inf] and right edges chosen to match the rule exactly). Check boundaries 2 and 24 explicitly. Show equivalence by running both versions on the same sample, including boundary and missing values, and asserting the Series are equal before replacing the old code.",
          "solutionFormat": "prose",
          "checks": [
            "Expresses the rule with np.select or pd.cut on whole columns.",
            "Handles the 2 and 24 hour boundaries and missing durations.",
            "Proves equivalence by comparing old and new results on the same data."
          ]
        },
