        "id": "correlation-and-outliers",
        "title": "14. Measure association and flag outliers with explicit rules",
        "stage": "intermediate",
        "takeaway": "Pearson correlation measures linear association and is pulled by a few extreme values; Spearman uses ranks. Flag outliers with a stated rule such as the IQR fences and report results with and without them.",
        "sections": [
          {
            "title": "Understand the decision",
            "paragraphs": [
              "A correlation coefficient summarises how two columns move together, from -1 to 1. Pearson's coefficient measures linear association and can be dominated by one extreme point. Spearman's coefficient correlates ranks, so it measures any monotonic relationship and resists outliers. Neither says one variable causes the other (lesson 18), and a near-zero value can hide a strong curved relationship, so plot the data first (lesson 16).",
              "Lesson 13 asked whether a long duration is real before excluding it. To flag candidates consistently, use a stated rule: the interquartile range (IQR) fences mark values below Q1 - 1.5 × IQR or above Q3 + 1.5 × IQR. Flag them in a column, investigate, and show the result with and without them; do not delete rows silently."
            ]
          },
          {
            "title": "Worked example",
            "paragraphs": [
              "Without the extreme ticket, replies and hours are strongly associated by both measures. One ticket with 400 hours drags Pearson's coefficient from 0.98 to 0.64, while Spearman stays at 0.99 because the ticket is still simply the largest rank. The IQR rule flags exactly that ticket."
            ],
            "example": "import pandas as pd\n\ndf = pd.DataFrame({\"replies\": [1, 2, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7],\n                   \"hours\":   [2, 3, 5, 6, 8, 9, 12, 400]})\nclean = df.iloc[:-1]\nprint(\"without outlier:\", round(clean.corr().loc[\"replies\", \"hours\"], 2),\n      round(clean.corr(method=\"spearman\").loc[\"replies\", \"hours\"], 2))\nprint(\"with outlier:   \", round(df.corr().loc[\"replies\", \"hours\"], 2),\n      round(df.corr(method=\"spearman\").loc[\"replies\", \"hours\"], 2))\n\nq1, q3 = df[\"hours\"].quantile([0.25, 0.75])\niqr = q3 - q1\ndf[\"outlier\"] = (df[\"hours\"] < q1 - 1.5 * iqr) | (df[\"hours\"] > q3 + 1.5 * iqr)\nprint(q1, q3, df.loc[df[\"outlier\"], \"hours\"].tolist())\n\n# Output:\n# without outlier: 0.98 0.99\n# with outlier:    0.64 0.99\n# 4.5 9.75 [400]"
          }
        ],
        "exercise": {
          "prompt": "A manager reads a Pearson correlation of 0.05 between ticket length (characters) and resolution hours and concludes that length does not matter. List what you would check before agreeing and how you would report the result.",
          "solution": "Plot length against hours to look for a curved or clustered relationship that Pearson misses. Compute Spearman as well. Flag outliers with the IQR rule and recompute both coefficients with and without them, because a few extreme tickets can hide or create an association. Check groups (priority, channel) separately, since mixing groups can cancel a relationship. Report the coefficients with their method and row count, the outlier rule and its effect, and state that correlation describes association in this data, not whether making tickets shorter would change resolution time.",
          "solutionFormat": "prose",
          "checks": [
            "Plots the data and compares Pearson with Spearman.",
            "Tests sensitivity to flagged outliers with a stated rule.",
            "Checks subgroups and avoids a causal conclusion."
          ]
        },
