        "id": "reproducible-notebooks",
        "title": "21. Keep notebooks reproducible",
        "stage": "advanced",
        "takeaway": "A notebook's kernel remembers every cell you ran, in the order you ran it; a result counts only if a fresh kernel reproduces it by running all cells from the top.",
        "sections": [
          {
            "title": "Understand the decision",
            "paragraphs": [
              "Jupyter notebooks are good for exploration: code, output, charts and notes sit together. The kernel, however, keeps every variable you created in any order. Re-running an earlier cell after a later one, deleting a cell whose variable still exists, or editing a cell without re-running its dependants all produce outputs that the saved notebook cannot reproduce.",
              "Before sharing a result, restart the kernel and run all cells, or execute the notebook from the command line (for example `jupyter nbconvert --to notebook --execute`). Read inputs from files with fixed paths and seeds, pin library versions, keep reusable transformations in a tested module (lesson 20) that the notebook imports, and clear large or sensitive outputs before committing. Use the notebook to explain; let the report command (lesson 23) produce the final numbers."
            ]
          },
          {
            "title": "Worked example",
            "paragraphs": [
              "The three cells are run once in the order a person might run them while exploring, and once top to bottom as a fresh kernel would. Re-running the conversion cell converts minutes to minutes again, so the exploratory total is 60 times too large, and nothing on screen says so. A restart and run-all exposes it."
            ],
            "example": "cells = [\n    \"durations = [2, 30, 50, 4]                       # hours, loaded from the file\",\n    \"durations = [d * 60 for d in durations]          # convert to minutes\",\n    \"total_minutes = sum(durations)\",\n]\n\ndef run(order):\n    kernel = {}                                       # the variables a kernel keeps\n    for index in order:\n        exec(cells[index], kernel)\n    return kernel[\"total_minutes\"]\n\nprint(\"exploration order 0,1,2,1,2:\", run([0, 1, 2, 1, 2]))\nprint(\"fresh kernel order 0,1,2:\", run([0, 1, 2]))\n\n# Output:\n# exploration order 0,1,2,1,2: 309600\n# fresh kernel order 0,1,2: 5160"
          }
        ],
