          "prompt": "Write a History class around Editor that keeps at most limit snapshots: checkpoint() saves the current state and drops the oldest beyond the limit; undo() restores the latest snapshot and returns False when none is left. Show that only the last two checkpoints can be restored with limit=2.",
          "solution": "class Editor:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self._text, self._cursor = \"\", 0\n\n    def type(self, chars):\n        self._text = self._text[:self._cursor] + chars + self._text[self._cursor:]\n        self._cursor += len(chars)\n\n    def save(self):\n        return (self._text, self._cursor)  # opaque snapshot: callers store it, never edit it\n\n    def restore(self, memento):\n        self._text, self._cursor = memento\n\n    def __str__(self):\n        return self._text\n\nclass History:\n    def __init__(self, editor, limit):\n        self.editor, self.limit, self.snapshots = editor, limit, []\n\n    def checkpoint(self):\n        self.snapshots.append(self.editor.save())\n        del self.snapshots[:-self.limit]\n\n    def undo(self):\n        if not self.snapshots:\n            return False\n        self.editor.restore(self.snapshots.pop())\n        return True\n\neditor = Editor()\nhistory = History(editor, limit=2)\nfor word in [\"one \", \"two \", \"three \"]:\n    history.checkpoint()\n    editor.type(word)\nprint(repr(str(editor)))\nprint(history.undo(), repr(str(editor)))\nprint(history.undo(), repr(str(editor)))\nprint(history.undo(), repr(str(editor)))",
