        "id": "repository-configuration",
        "title": "23. Configure hooks, attributes, worktrees and signed commits",
        "stage": "advanced",
        "takeaway": "Hooks and attributes encode team rules in the repository, worktrees let you work on two branches at once, and signatures prove who made a commit.",
        "sections": [
          {
            "title": "Hooks and attributes",
            "paragraphs": [
              "Hooks are scripts Git runs at points such as `pre-commit`, `commit-msg` and `pre-push`. A non-zero exit stops the operation. Hooks in `.git/hooks` are not committed, so teams keep them in a tracked folder and set `core.hooksPath`, or use a hook manager. Hooks run on each developer's machine and can be skipped with `--no-verify`, so anything that must hold also runs in CI.",
              "`.gitattributes` assigns attributes to paths. `text eol=lf` normalises line endings for shell scripts so they run on Linux even when edited on Windows. `binary` marks files that should never be diffed or merged as text. `filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text` hands large files to Git LFS, which stores a small pointer in the repository and the content on an LFS server. `git check-attr` shows what applies to a path.",
              "Submodules embed another repository at a fixed commit. They suit vendored dependencies with their own history, but every clone needs `--recurse-submodules` and updates need a deliberate commit, so a package manager is usually simpler."
            ],
            "example": "# .githooks/pre-commit (tracked), enabled with: git config core.hooksPath .githooks\n#!/bin/sh\nif git diff --cached | grep -q \"^+.*DO NOT COMMIT\"; then\n  echo \"pre-commit: remove DO NOT COMMIT markers\" >&2\n  exit 1\nfi\n\ngit commit -m \"Try\"          # with a staged DO NOT COMMIT line\n# pre-commit: remove DO NOT COMMIT markers   (exit 1, no commit created)\n\n# .gitattributes\n*.sh  text eol=lf\n*.png binary\n*.psd filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text\n\ngit check-attr eol diff filter -- deploy.sh logo.png art.psd\n# deploy.sh: eol: lf\n# deploy.sh: diff: unspecified\n# deploy.sh: filter: unspecified\n# logo.png: eol: unspecified\n# logo.png: diff: unset\n# logo.png: filter: unspecified\n# art.psd: eol: unspecified\n# art.psd: diff: lfs\n# art.psd: filter: lfs"
          },
          {
            "title": "Worktrees and signing",
            "paragraphs": [
              "`git worktree add ../app-hotfix -b hotfix` checks out a second branch in another folder that shares the same repository and objects. You can fix production in one folder while a long build or a half-finished change stays untouched in the other, without stashing. A branch can be checked out in only one worktree at a time. Remove finished ones with `git worktree remove`.",
              "Anyone can set `user.name` and `user.email` to any value, so a commit's author field proves nothing. Signing a commit or tag with a GPG or SSH key (`git config gpg.format ssh`, `user.signingkey`, `commit.gpgsign true`) lets others verify it with `git log --show-signature` or `git verify-tag`, and hosting platforms can require signed commits on protected branches. Keep signing keys protected like any other credential."
            ],
            "example": "git worktree add -b hotfix ../plumbing-hotfix\ngit -C ../plumbing-hotfix branch --show-current\n# hotfix\ngit worktree list --porcelain | grep ^branch\n# branch refs/heads/main\n# branch refs/heads/hotfix\ngit worktree remove ../plumbing-hotfix\n\ngit config gpg.format ssh\ngit config user.signingkey ~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub\ngit commit -S -m \"Sign this commit\"\ngit log --show-signature -1"
          }
        ],
        "exercise": {
          "prompt": "A repository has Windows and Linux developers. Shell scripts sometimes fail in CI with `/bin/sh^M: bad interpreter`, 200 MB design files make clones slow, and a pre-commit secret scanner is installed by only half the team. Propose a change for each problem.",
          "solution": "Line endings: add `*.sh text eol=lf` to .gitattributes and renormalise once with `git add --renormalize .`, so scripts are stored and checked out with LF.\nLarge files: track design files with Git LFS (`git lfs track \"*.psd\"`, which writes the .gitattributes filter line) and, if history must shrink, migrate old versions with `git lfs migrate` after agreeing a rewrite with the team.\nSecret scanning: keep the hook in a tracked .githooks folder with `core.hooksPath` documented in setup, and run the same scanner in CI as a required check, because local hooks can be skipped.",
          "solutionFormat": "prose",
          "checks": [
            "Uses .gitattributes eol=lf for shell scripts.",
            "Moves large binaries to Git LFS.",
            "Backs up a local hook with a required CI check."
          ]
        },
        "quiz": {
          "question": "Why must a rule enforced by a local pre-commit hook also run in CI?",
          "options": [
            "Hooks only run on Linux",
            "Hooks are not installed automatically and can be skipped with --no-verify",
            "CI cannot read .gitattributes"
          ],
          "correct": 1,
          "explanation": "Hooks live on each machine and are optional. A required CI check is the enforcement point everyone passes through."
        },
