        "id": "generics-utility-types",
        "stage": "foundation",
        "title": "8. Generics, utility types and compiler settings",
        "takeaway": "A generic keeps the relationship between input and output types; utility types derive new shapes from one source type instead of copying it.",
        "sections": [
          {
            "title": "Generic functions",
            "paragraphs": [
              "A type parameter such as `<T>` lets one function work with many types while remembering which one it received: `first(lessons)` returns `Lesson | undefined`, not `any`. TypeScript usually infers `T` from the arguments.",
              "A constraint limits what `T` can be: `T extends { id: string }` lets the body read `item.id`. `K extends keyof T` restricts a key to the actual property names, and `T[K]` is the type of that property, so `pluck(lessons, \"minutes\")` is `number[]` and a misspelt key fails to compile."
            ],
            "example": "function first<T>(items: readonly T[]): T | undefined {\n  return items[0];\n}\nfunction byId<T extends { id: string }>(items: readonly T[]): Map<string, T> {\n  return new Map(items.map(item => [item.id, item]));\n}\nfunction pluck<T, K extends keyof T>(items: readonly T[], key: K): T[K][] {\n  return items.map(item => item[key]);\n}\n\ntype Lesson = { id: string; title: string; minutes: number };\nconst lessons: Lesson[] = [\n  { id: \"a\", title: \"Types\", minutes: 20 },\n  { id: \"b\", title: \"Generics\", minutes: 35 },\n];\nconsole.log(first(lessons)?.title);          // first returns Lesson | undefined\nconsole.log(byId(lessons).get(\"b\")?.minutes);\nconst minutes = pluck(lessons, \"minutes\");   // number[]\nconsole.log(minutes);\n// pluck(lessons, \"owner\"); // compile error: \"owner\" is not a key of Lesson\n// Output:\n// Types\n// 35\n// [ 20, 35 ]"
          },
          {
            "title": "Utility types",
            "paragraphs": [
              "Utility types build a new type from an existing one. `Partial<T>` makes every property optional, `Required<T>` the reverse, `Pick<T, K>` keeps some properties and `Omit<T, K>` drops them. `Record<K, V>` describes an object with exactly the keys `K`, and `Readonly<T>` blocks assignment through that reference.",
              "`ReturnType<typeof fn>` and `Awaited<T>` read types back out of functions and promises. Deriving from one source type means a renamed field shows up as compile errors everywhere it is used, instead of drifting between hand-written copies."
            ],
            "example": "type Lesson = { id: string; title: string; minutes: number; done: boolean };\ntype LessonPatch = Partial<Omit<Lesson, \"id\">>;   // any field except id, all optional\ntype Summary = Pick<Lesson, \"id\" | \"title\">;\ntype Status = \"todo\" | \"doing\" | \"done\";\n\nfunction update(lesson: Readonly<Lesson>, patch: LessonPatch): Lesson {\n  return { ...lesson, ...patch };\n}\ntype Updated = ReturnType<typeof update>;         // Lesson\n\nconst counts: Record<Status, number> = { todo: 2, doing: 1, done: 4 };\nconst lesson: Lesson = { id: \"a\", title: \"Types\", minutes: 20, done: false };\nconst changed: Updated = update(lesson, { done: true });\nconst summary: Summary = { id: changed.id, title: changed.title };\nconsole.log(changed.done, lesson.done, summary, counts.done);\n// Output:\n// true false { id: 'a', title: 'Types' } 4"
          },
          {
            "title": "as const and satisfies",
            "paragraphs": [
              "`as const` keeps literal values (`\"/\"` rather than `string`) and makes the object readonly. `satisfies` checks a value against a type without widening it, so `routes` is validated as a map of paths while `keyof typeof routes` is still the exact union of its names."
            ],
            "example": "const routes = {\n  home: \"/\",\n  lesson: \"/lessons/:id\",\n} as const satisfies Record<string, `/${string}`>;\n\ntype RouteName = keyof typeof routes;  // \"home\" | \"lesson\"\nconst path = (name: RouteName) => routes[name];\nconsole.log(path(\"lesson\"));\n// path(\"settings\");              // compile error: not a RouteName\n// routes.home = \"/start\";        // compile error: as const makes it readonly\n// Output:\n// /lessons/:id"
          },
          {
            "title": "Compiler settings and limits",
            "paragraphs": [
              "`strict: true` turns on the checks this course relies on, including `strictNullChecks` and `noImplicitAny`. `noUncheckedIndexedAccess` makes `items[i]` include `undefined`, matching what JavaScript does for a missing index. Run `tsc --noEmit` in CI: bundlers and Node's type stripping remove types without checking them.",
              "Types disappear at runtime. A generic `parse<T>(text)` that casts its result proves nothing about the data; validate unknown input as in the narrowing lesson, then let the types describe what you have checked."
            ]
          }
        ],
        "exercise": {
          "prompt": "Write groupBy<T, K extends PropertyKey>(items, keyOf) returning Partial<Record<K, T[]>>. Group three lessons by their \"todo\" | \"done\" status and print the done IDs and the number of todo lessons.",
          "solution": "function groupBy<T, K extends PropertyKey>(\n  items: readonly T[],\n  keyOf: (item: T) => K\n): Partial<Record<K, T[]>> {\n  const groups: Partial<Record<K, T[]>> = {};\n  for (const item of items) {\n    (groups[keyOf(item)] ??= []).push(item);\n  }\n  return groups;\n}\n\ntype Lesson = { id: string; status: \"todo\" | \"done\" };\nconst lessons: Lesson[] = [\n  { id: \"a\", status: \"done\" },\n  { id: \"b\", status: \"todo\" },\n  { id: \"c\", status: \"done\" },\n];\nconst groups = groupBy(lessons, lesson => lesson.status);\nconsole.log(groups.done?.map(lesson => lesson.id), groups.todo?.length);\n// Output:\n// [ 'a', 'c' ] 1",
          "checks": [
            "groups.done has type Lesson[] | undefined, so reading it needs ?. or a check.",
            "Passing a keyOf that returns an object fails to compile, because K must be a property key.",
            "tsc --noEmit reports no errors with strict enabled."
          ]
        },
