        "id": "workload-types",
        "title": "20. Choose StatefulSet, DaemonSet or CronJob for the workload",
        "stage": "advanced",
        "takeaway": "Deployments suit interchangeable Pods; StatefulSets give stable identity and storage, DaemonSets run one Pod per node, and CronJobs start Jobs on a schedule.",
        "sections": [
          {
            "title": "Identity, placement and time",
            "paragraphs": [
              "A Deployment (lesson 6) treats Pods as interchangeable: any replica can be replaced by a new one with a new name and empty scratch space. Some workloads need more. A StatefulSet gives each Pod a stable ordinal name (`queue-0`, `queue-1`), a stable DNS name through a headless Service, and its own PersistentVolumeClaim from `volumeClaimTemplates` that follows that name when the Pod is rescheduled. Pods start, update and stop in order by default. Use it for clustered databases, brokers and anything whose members must find each other by name.",
              "A DaemonSet runs one Pod on every node, or every node that matches a selector, and adds one when a node joins. Node agents fit here: log shippers, metrics collectors, network and storage plugins. They often need host access, so review their privileges carefully (lesson 17).",
              "A CronJob creates a Job (lesson 19) on a cron schedule. Set `timeZone` explicitly, `concurrencyPolicy: Forbid` or `Replace` when overlapping runs would conflict, `startingDeadlineSeconds` so a missed schedule is skipped instead of run late, and history limits so finished Jobs are cleaned up. The Job itself must still be idempotent, because a run can be retried or, rarely, started twice."
            ],
            "example": "apiVersion: v1\nkind: Service\nmetadata:\n  name: queue\nspec:\n  clusterIP: None              # headless: DNS returns each Pod, not one virtual IP\n  selector: {app: queue}\n  ports: [{name: amqp, port: 5672}]\n---\napiVersion: apps/v1\nkind: StatefulSet\nmetadata:\n  name: queue\nspec:\n  serviceName: queue           # Pods get stable names: queue-0.queue, queue-1.queue, ...\n  replicas: 3\n  selector:\n    matchLabels: {app: queue}\n  template:\n    metadata:\n      labels: {app: queue}\n    spec:\n      containers:\n      - name: broker\n        image: registry.example.test/queue:3.13.2\n        ports: [{name: amqp, containerPort: 5672}]\n        volumeMounts: [{name: data, mountPath: /var/lib/queue}]\n  volumeClaimTemplates:        # one PersistentVolumeClaim per Pod: data-queue-0, data-queue-1, ...\n  - metadata: {name: data}\n    spec:\n      accessModes: [ReadWriteOnce]\n      resources: {requests: {storage: 10Gi}}\n---\napiVersion: apps/v1\nkind: DaemonSet\nmetadata:\n  name: log-agent\nspec:\n  selector:\n    matchLabels: {app: log-agent}\n  template:\n    metadata:\n      labels: {app: log-agent}\n    spec:\n      tolerations: [{operator: Exists}]       # also run on tainted nodes\n      containers:\n      - name: agent\n        image: registry.example.test/log-agent:1.4.2\n        volumeMounts: [{name: logs, mountPath: /var/log, readOnly: true}]\n      volumes: [{name: logs, hostPath: {path: /var/log}}]\n---\napiVersion: batch/v1\nkind: CronJob\nmetadata:\n  name: nightly-report\nspec:\n  schedule: \"30 2 * * *\"       # 02:30 every day\n  timeZone: Europe/London\n  concurrencyPolicy: Forbid    # skip a run while the previous one is still going\n  startingDeadlineSeconds: 600\n  successfulJobsHistoryLimit: 3\n  failedJobsHistoryLimit: 3\n  jobTemplate:\n    spec:\n      backoffLimit: 2\n      template:\n        spec:\n          restartPolicy: Never\n          containers:\n          - name: report\n            image: registry.example.test/report:2.0.1\n            args: [\"--date\", \"yesterday\"]"
          },
          {
            "title": "What a StatefulSet does not do",
            "paragraphs": [
              "A StatefulSet keeps names and volumes stable; it does not replicate data, elect leaders or back anything up. The application inside must handle clustering, and deleting or scaling down a StatefulSet does not delete its claims by default, so storage and its cost remain until you remove them on purpose (lesson 18). Managed database services are often a better choice than running stateful systems yourself.",
              "These manifests were checked offline for consistent selectors, the headless Service name, volume names and a five-field schedule. They were not applied to a cluster."
            ]
          }
        ],
        "exercise": {
          "prompt": "For each workload, choose Deployment, StatefulSet, DaemonSet, Job or CronJob and give the deciding property: a stateless web API, a three-member etcd cluster, a node-level metrics exporter, a one-off database migration, a weekly cleanup of expired sessions.",
          "solution": "Web API: Deployment, because replicas are interchangeable. etcd cluster: StatefulSet, because members need stable names and their own persistent volumes. Metrics exporter: DaemonSet, because one Pod must run on every node. Database migration: Job, because it runs once to completion with bounded retries. Weekly cleanup: CronJob with concurrencyPolicy Forbid, an explicit time zone and an idempotent cleanup.",
          "solutionFormat": "prose",
          "checks": [
            "Matches interchangeable replicas to a Deployment.",
            "Uses a StatefulSet for stable identity and storage.",
            "Uses a DaemonSet for per-node agents and Job/CronJob for finite and scheduled work."
          ]
        },
        "quiz": {
          "question": "Which object gives each Pod its own PersistentVolumeClaim that follows the Pod's name?",
          "options": [
            "Deployment",
            "StatefulSet with volumeClaimTemplates",
            "DaemonSet"
          ],
          "correct": 1,
          "explanation": "volumeClaimTemplates create one claim per StatefulSet ordinal, reattached when that Pod is recreated."
        },
