        "id": "quotas-and-extensions",
        "title": "25. Set namespace guard rails and extend the API with custom resources",
        "stage": "advanced",
        "takeaway": "ResourceQuota caps a namespace's total use and LimitRange sets per-container defaults; custom resources plus a controller let you manage new kinds of things the Kubernetes way.",
        "sections": [
          {
            "title": "Quotas and limit ranges",
            "paragraphs": [
              "When several teams share a cluster, one namespace can starve the others. A ResourceQuota caps the namespace's totals: requested CPU and memory, limits, number of Pods, claims or Services. Once a quota covers CPU or memory, every new Pod in the namespace must declare those requests, or admission rejects it.",
              "A LimitRange sets defaults and bounds per container or Pod. It fills in a default request and limit when a manifest omits them, which lets quota-covered namespaces accept simple manifests, and it rejects containers asking for more than the maximum. Requests and limits themselves were covered in lesson 11.",
              "Quotas are checked at admission, when objects are created; they do not stop a running process from using memory up to its limit. Combine them with RBAC (lesson 16) so teams cannot edit their own quotas."
            ],
            "example": "apiVersion: v1\nkind: ResourceQuota\nmetadata:\n  name: team-budget\n  namespace: notebook-lab\nspec:\n  hard:\n    requests.cpu: \"4\"\n    requests.memory: 8Gi\n    limits.memory: 12Gi\n    pods: \"30\"\n    persistentvolumeclaims: \"5\"\n---\napiVersion: v1\nkind: LimitRange\nmetadata:\n  name: container-defaults\n  namespace: notebook-lab\nspec:\n  limits:\n  - type: Container\n    defaultRequest: {cpu: 100m, memory: 128Mi}   # applied when a container sets no request\n    default: {memory: 256Mi}                     # applied when a container sets no limit\n    max: {memory: 2Gi}\n---\napiVersion: apiextensions.k8s.io/v1\nkind: CustomResourceDefinition\nmetadata:\n  name: backupschedules.ops.example.test\nspec:\n  group: ops.example.test\n  scope: Namespaced\n  names: {plural: backupschedules, singular: backupschedule, kind: BackupSchedule}\n  versions:\n  - name: v1\n    served: true\n    storage: true\n    schema:\n      openAPIV3Schema:\n        type: object\n        properties:\n          spec:\n            type: object\n            required: [database, schedule]\n            properties:\n              database: {type: string}\n              schedule: {type: string}\n              keepDays: {type: integer, minimum: 1, maximum: 90}\n---\napiVersion: ops.example.test/v1\nkind: BackupSchedule\nmetadata:\n  name: orders-nightly\n  namespace: notebook-lab\nspec:\n  database: orders\n  schedule: \"0 1 * * *\"\n  keepDays: 14"
          },
          {
            "title": "Custom resources and operators",
            "paragraphs": [
              "A CustomResourceDefinition adds a new kind to the API: once applied, `kubectl get backupschedules` works, objects are validated against the OpenAPI schema, and RBAC applies as for built-in kinds. A custom resource is only data. Something has to act on it.",
              "An operator is a controller that watches custom resources and reconciles the real world toward them, exactly as the Deployment controller does for Pods (lesson 1). A database operator can create StatefulSets, run backups and perform failover from a short custom resource. Operators concentrate operational knowledge, but they run with broad permissions, and a buggy one can act on every resource it watches, so review their RBAC and upgrade them carefully.",
              "The manifests were checked offline: the CRD's name matches its plural and group, and the BackupSchedule matches the CRD's group, version, kind and required fields. They were not applied to a cluster."
            ]
          }
        ],
        "exercise": {
          "prompt": "A namespace has a ResourceQuota on requests.cpu and requests.memory. A developer's Deployment with no resources section creates no Pods, and its ReplicaSet reports 'failed quota: must specify requests.cpu'. Explain the cause and two ways to fix it, saying which is better for the team.",
          "solution": "Once a quota covers CPU and memory requests, admission rejects Pods that do not declare them, so the ReplicaSet cannot create any. Fix 1: add resources.requests (and limits) to the Deployment, which is the better long-term practice because the values reflect the workload. Fix 2: add a LimitRange with defaultRequest and default values so omitted requests are filled in automatically, which is a good safety net for the namespace. Use both: the LimitRange prevents surprise failures, and explicit requests keep scheduling accurate.",
          "solutionFormat": "prose",
          "checks": [
            "Explains that quota-covered resources must be declared.",
            "Offers explicit requests and a LimitRange default.",
            "Notes the ReplicaSet events as the evidence."
          ]
        },
        "quiz": {
          "question": "What happens when you apply a CustomResourceDefinition and create a custom resource, but no operator is installed?",
          "options": [
            "Kubernetes runs the resource with default behaviour",
            "The resource is stored and validated, but nothing acts on it",
            "The API server refuses to store it"
          ],
          "correct": 1,
          "explanation": "A CRD only extends the API. Behaviour comes from a controller that watches and reconciles the custom resources."
        },
