        "id": "trees-and-ensembles",
        "title": "14. Split on features with trees and ensembles",
        "stage": "intermediate",
        "takeaway": "One deep tree memorises; averaging many trees or adding small trees step by step generalises better, if you limit their capacity.",
        "sections": [
          {
            "title": "How it works",
            "paragraphs": [
              "A decision tree asks a sequence of yes/no questions about single features, such as `priority = high` or `customer_messages > 3`. Each split is chosen to make the two groups purer in the target. Prediction follows the questions to a leaf and returns that leaf's majority class or mean value. Trees need no scaling, handle mixed feature types and capture interactions and non-linear boundaries that a linear model cannot.",
              "A tree grown until every leaf is pure fits the training rows perfectly, including their noise. Depth limits (`max_depth`), a minimum leaf size (`min_samples_leaf`) and pruning control that capacity, just as `C` did for logistic regression in lesson 13.",
              "A random forest trains many deep trees, each on a bootstrap sample of rows and considering a random subset of features at each split, then averages their votes. Averaging reduces variance. Gradient boosting instead adds small trees one at a time, each fitted to the errors the current ensemble still makes, scaled down by a learning rate. Boosting is often the strongest choice for tabular data, but it can overfit if it runs too many steps, so use early stopping or choose the number of steps by cross-validation."
            ]
          },
          {
            "title": "Worked example",
            "paragraphs": [
              "The data are two interleaved, noisy half-circles, so a straight boundary cannot separate them. All models use the same 420 training and 180 test rows. The unlimited tree and default boosting reach 1.000 on training rows but not on test rows. The depth-4 tree, the forest and boosting with early stopping keep a smaller gap.",
              "On 180 test rows one prediction is about 0.006 of accuracy, so the differences between 0.844 and 0.883 are a handful of rows. Choose among such candidates with cross-validation on training data (lesson 12), not by picking the best test score."
            ],
            "example": "from sklearn.datasets import make_moons\nfrom sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split\nfrom sklearn.linear_model import LogisticRegression\nfrom sklearn.tree import DecisionTreeClassifier\nfrom sklearn.ensemble import RandomForestClassifier, HistGradientBoostingClassifier\n\nX, y = make_moons(n_samples=600, noise=0.3, random_state=0)   # two interleaved, noisy half-circles\nX_train, X_test, y_train, y_test = train_test_split(X, y, test_size=0.3, random_state=0, stratify=y)\nmodels = {\n    'logistic regression': LogisticRegression(),\n    'tree, no depth limit': DecisionTreeClassifier(random_state=0),\n    'tree, max_depth=4': DecisionTreeClassifier(max_depth=4, random_state=0),\n    'random forest': RandomForestClassifier(n_estimators=200, min_samples_leaf=3, random_state=0),\n    'boosting, defaults': HistGradientBoostingClassifier(random_state=0),\n    'boosting, early stop': HistGradientBoostingClassifier(max_leaf_nodes=8, learning_rate=0.05, max_iter=200,\n                                                           early_stopping=True, random_state=0),\n}\nfor name, model in models.items():\n    model.fit(X_train, y_train)\n    print(f'{name:21} train {model.score(X_train, y_train):.3f}  test {model.score(X_test, y_test):.3f}')\n\n# Output:\n# logistic regression   train 0.879  test 0.844\n# tree, no depth limit  train 1.000  test 0.839\n# tree, max_depth=4     train 0.905  test 0.883\n# random forest         train 0.964  test 0.867\n# boosting, defaults    train 1.000  test 0.839\n# boosting, early stop  train 0.945  test 0.861"
          },
          {
            "title": "Check the assumption",
            "paragraphs": [
              "Trees split on thresholds, so they cannot extrapolate: a regression tree never predicts beyond the range of training targets. If future values may exceed anything seen, check that limit before choosing one.",
              "Ensembles cost more to train, store and explain than one linear model. Start with the simple baseline and a regularised linear model, and adopt an ensemble when validation evidence shows a gain that matters for the decision. Tree-based models still need the same leakage and time-split discipline as any other model."
            ]
          }
        ],
