        "id": "distance-and-clustering",
        "title": "16. Predict from neighbours and find groups without labels",
        "stage": "intermediate",
        "takeaway": "Distance-based methods depend on feature scale; clusters are a description to check, not a discovered truth.",
        "sections": [
          {
            "title": "How it works",
            "paragraphs": [
              "k-nearest neighbours stores the training rows. To predict, it finds the k rows closest to the new one and takes their majority class or mean value. There is no fitted formula, so prediction cost grows with the training set. Small k follows noise; large k smooths the boundary.",
              "Every distance-based method compares features in their own units. A column measured in thousands dominates one measured in units, whatever its predictive value. Scale features inside the pipeline (lesson 11), so the scaler learns its means and spreads from training rows only. Distances also become less informative as the number of features grows.",
              "Clustering is unsupervised: there is no target. k-means chooses k centres and assigns each row to its nearest centre, then moves each centre to the mean of its rows, repeating until assignments stop changing. It favours compact, similarly sized, round groups. Because it starts from random centres, run several starts (`n_init`) and fix `random_state` for reproducibility."
            ]
          },
          {
            "title": "Worked example",
            "paragraphs": [
              "The classification data have two informative columns and one uninformative column multiplied by 1000. Unscaled k-NN mostly measures that column and scores 0.564 in cross-validation, close to guessing. Scaling inside the pipeline restores 0.924.",
              "The clustering data were generated as three groups, yet the silhouette score, which rewards tight, well-separated clusters, prefers two. Two of the generated groups sit close together. Two k-means runs with different seeds give the same grouping with different label numbers: the adjusted Rand index of 1.0 shows the partitions match exactly."
            ],
            "example": "from sklearn.datasets import make_classification\nfrom sklearn.model_selection import cross_val_score\nfrom sklearn.neighbors import KNeighborsClassifier\nfrom sklearn.pipeline import make_pipeline\nfrom sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler\n\nX, y = make_classification(n_samples=500, n_features=4, n_informative=2, n_redundant=0, random_state=1)\nX[:, 2] *= 1000          # an uninformative column recorded in much larger units\nfor name, model in [('k-NN, raw units', KNeighborsClassifier(n_neighbors=15)),\n                    ('k-NN, scaled', make_pipeline(StandardScaler(), KNeighborsClassifier(n_neighbors=15)))]:\n    print(f'{name:16} CV accuracy {cross_val_score(model, X, y, cv=5).mean():.3f}')\n\n# Output:\n# k-NN, raw units  CV accuracy 0.564\n# k-NN, scaled     CV accuracy 0.924\n\nfrom sklearn.datasets import make_blobs\nfrom sklearn.cluster import KMeans\nfrom sklearn.metrics import silhouette_score, adjusted_rand_score\n\nX, _ = make_blobs(n_samples=300, centers=3, cluster_std=1.0, random_state=4)   # generated as 3 groups\nfor k in range(2, 6):\n    labels = KMeans(n_clusters=k, n_init=10, random_state=0).fit_predict(X)\n    print(f'k={k} silhouette {silhouette_score(X, labels):.3f}')\na = KMeans(n_clusters=3, n_init=10, random_state=0).fit_predict(X)\nb = KMeans(n_clusters=3, n_init=10, random_state=1).fit_predict(X)\nprint('first labels', a[:6], b[:6])\nprint('same grouping (adjusted Rand index):', round(adjusted_rand_score(a, b), 3))\n\n# Output:\n# k=2 silhouette 0.749\n# k=3 silhouette 0.631\n# k=4 silhouette 0.538\n# k=5 silhouette 0.387\n# first labels [0 1 1 2 2 1] [1 0 0 2 2 0]\n# same grouping (adjusted Rand index): 1.0"
          },
          {
            "title": "Check the assumption",
            "paragraphs": [
              "Cluster numbers are arbitrary names. Compare clusterings with a label-free measure such as the adjusted Rand index, and describe each cluster by its feature averages before giving it a business meaning.",
              "There is no ground truth to score against, so judge a clustering by stability (do the groups survive a different seed or sample?) and usefulness (does treating the groups differently help a decision?). A silhouette score is a guide to compactness, not proof that the groups are real. Density-based methods such as DBSCAN suit irregular shapes and can label rows as noise; k-means cannot."
            ]
          }
        ],
        "exercise": {
          "prompt": "A colleague clusters customers with k-means on annual spend (in currency units, up to 50,000) and number of support tickets (0 to 20), and reports that tickets do not matter to the clusters. What went wrong, and how would you redo the analysis?",
          "solution": "Spend is on a scale thousands of times larger, so Euclidean distance is almost entirely spend and the ticket count barely moves any row between clusters. Scale both features (for example with StandardScaler in a pipeline), rerun k-means with several starts and a fixed seed, compare several values of k with silhouette and stability across seeds or resamples, and describe each cluster by its average spend and tickets before interpreting it.",
          "solutionFormat": "prose",
          "checks": [
            "Identifies feature scale as the cause.",
            "Scales features before clustering.",
            "Checks k and stability instead of trusting one run.",
            "Describes clusters by feature averages before naming them."
          ]
        },
