        "id": "trace-spans",
        "title": "7. Traces, spans and the critical path",
        "takeaway": "Nested timing describes dependency relationships, not an additive bill.",
        "stage": "foundation",
        "sections": [
          {
            "title": "Span relationships and overlap",
            "paragraphs": [
              "A trace groups spans for a logical operation. Each span records a start, end and operation name; a root span has no parent, while a child refers to its parent. In the example below the request waits for its parsing and database work, so the child intervals fit inside the request span. Adding those durations to the parent double-counts elapsed time. In asynchronous systems, a child can outlive its parent: inspect the actual intervals rather than assume containment.",
              "Parallel children can overlap. A parent lasting 120 ms with two overlapping 80 ms children does not imply 160 ms of user waiting. Study the timeline and critical path: the chain of dependencies that determines completion. Missing instrumentation leaves unaccounted time, which is a question to investigate rather than proof of CPU work."
            ],
            "example": "Request span: [0,120] ms\nParse span: [5,15] ms\nQuery A: [20,100] ms; Query B: [25,105] ms\nChildren overlap; do not sum them into request latency."
          },
          {
            "title": "Missing work and sampled traces",
            "paragraphs": [
              "A trace may omit queued work, retries or spans excluded by sampling. Check where instrumentation starts and stops, then use logs and aggregate metrics to investigate the gaps."
            ]
          },
          {
            "title": "Compute overlap before assigning the missing time",
            "paragraphs": [
              "A parent spans 0 to 150 ms. Child A spans 10 to 100; B spans 10 to 130. Their durations sum to 210 ms, but their union covers only 120 ms because 90 ms overlap. The remaining 30 ms belongs outside the measured child intervals. It may include setup, scheduling, serialization or uninstrumented waits; elapsed subtraction alone cannot call it CPU work.",
              "`trace_investigation.py` measures two real concurrent sleeping operations with a monotonic clock, prints their intervals, overlap, process CPU and uncovered parent time, then repeats with B reporting failure. It retains the failed span and request outcome rather than dropping failed requests from the latency story. These are manual spans without context propagation or an OpenTelemetry collector."
            ],
            "example": "A duration=90ms; B duration=120ms; overlap=90ms\ncovered child time=90+120-90=120ms\nuncovered parent=150-120=30ms\nlatest required child B determines completion before final assembly",
            "exampleFormat": "prose"
          }
        ],
