        "id": "book-most-sessions",
        "title": "37. Fit the most optional sessions",
        "stage": "advanced",
        "takeaway": "stretch · Python 3.14+ · Test your solution locally",
        "sections": [
          {
            "title": "The problem",
            "paragraphs": [
              "Each `[start, end]` is an optional half-open session. Choose as many sessions as possible without overlaps and return the count. A session may start exactly when the previous one ends. All sessions have equal value; their durations may differ. Identical rows represent competing sessions and cannot both be attended.",
              "Return a value from `book_most_sessions`; do not read input or print the answer. Keep the supplied arguments unchanged."
            ]
          },
          {
            "title": "Examples",
            "paragraphs": [
              "The calls below show the expected return values. Indexes, where used, start at zero."
            ],
            "example": "book_most_sessions([[0, 8], [1, 3], [3, 5], [5, 7]])\nExpected: 3\n\nbook_most_sessions([])\nExpected: 0"
          },
          {
            "title": "Constraints and edge cases",
            "paragraphs": [
              "Use Python 3.14 or newer. Do not change the input. Integer inputs never include `bool` values. Unless stated otherwise, a list has at most 1,000 elements and a string has at most 1,000 characters.",
              "Each row contains integer times with `0 <= start < end <= 1_000_000`. Input may be unsorted."
            ]
          }
        ],
        "exercise": {
          "challenge": true,
          "prompt": "Complete `book_most_sessions` below and run the tests. You can also edit `solutions.py` in the downloadable practice ZIP and use the terminal command shown below.",
          "starter": "def book_most_sessions(sessions: list[list[int]]) -> int:\n    raise NotImplementedError(\"Write your solution here\")",
          "checks": [
            "Match the expected return values, including edge cases.",
            "Do not modify the input arguments.",
            "A passing finite test set is evidence for these cases, not a proof for every valid input."
          ],
          "command": "python check.py book-most-sessions",
          "hints": [
            "Choosing the earliest starting session can block many shorter sessions.",
            "Among currently compatible sessions, the one ending earliest leaves the most remaining time.",
            "Sort by end time; accept a session when its start is at least the last accepted end."
          ],
          "solution": "def book_most_sessions(sessions: list[list[int]]) -> int:\n    count = 0\n    last_end = 0\n    for start, end in sorted(sessions, key=lambda interval: (interval[1], interval[0])):\n        if start >= last_end:\n            count += 1\n            last_end = end\n    return count\n",
          "reasoning": [
            "Consider the first session of any optimal schedule. Replacing it with the session that ends earliest cannot block a later session, because the replacement finishes no later. This preserves the achievable count.",
            "After accepting that session, the same argument applies to compatible sessions that remain. Sorting by end time lets one forward scan make those choices. This reasoning depends on maximizing count; it does not solve a version with unequal rewards."
          ],
          "complexity": "O(n log(n + 1)) time and O(n) sorting space. The selection scan itself uses O(1) auxiliary state.",
          "pitfalls": [
            "Choosing shortest duration is not justified by the earliest-finish argument.",
            "Using this greedy rule for unequal session rewards can miss the best total reward."
          ],
          "transfer": "Give sessions rewards: [0,2] rewards 1, [2,4] rewards 1, and [0,4] rewards 5. Earliest finish earns 2, while the long session earns 5. Define a state that compares taking and skipping sessions, then use exhaustive subsets as an oracle for small inputs."
        },
