        "id": "refactoring-and-debt",
        "title": "21. Refactor safely and manage technical debt",
        "stage": "advanced",
        "takeaway": "Refactoring changes structure without changing behaviour, in small steps under tests; technical debt is worth paying down where it slows the changes you are actually making.",
        "sections": [
          {
            "title": "The mechanism",
            "paragraphs": [
              "A refactoring is a change to internal structure that leaves observable behaviour unchanged: rename, extract a function, replace a condition with a named rule. Do it in small steps, run the tests after each, and commit refactorings separately from behaviour changes so a reviewer (lesson 15) can check each kind on its own terms and history (lesson 18) can isolate a regression.",
              "Before refactoring unfamiliar code, pin its current behaviour with characterization tests (lesson 8), including the bugs you have not yet agreed to fix. A preparatory refactoring reshapes the code so the next change becomes easy, then the behaviour change follows in its own commit: \"make the change easy, then make the easy change\".",
              "Technical debt is the extra cost future changes pay because of past shortcuts or outdated design. Some debt is deliberate and prudent (ship now, clean up after the release); some is accidental. Track it as concrete items with the cost they cause (\"every status rule change touches four functions\") and pay it down where you are already working, rather than in large unscheduled rewrites."
            ]
          },
          {
            "title": "Worked case",
            "paragraphs": [
              "The legacy summary repeats the status test and hides the rule. The refactored version names the rule once. The loop checks both versions agree on the existing cases, including the zero-minute ticket the legacy code skips: that bug is preserved here and fixed later in a separate, tested commit."
            ],
            "example": "def legacy_summary(rows):\n    total, count = 0, 0\n    for r in rows:\n        if r[\"status\"] == \"open\" or r[\"status\"] == \"pending\":\n            if r[\"minutes\"]:\n                total = total + int(r[\"minutes\"])\n                count = count + 1\n    return {\"count\": count, \"minutes\": total}\n\nACTIVE = {\"open\", \"pending\"}\n\ndef is_active(row):\n    return row[\"status\"] in ACTIVE\n\ndef summary(rows):                      # same behaviour, clearer structure\n    active = [r for r in rows if is_active(r) and r[\"minutes\"]]\n    return {\"count\": len(active), \"minutes\": sum(int(r[\"minutes\"]) for r in active)}\n\ncases = [\n    [],\n    [{\"status\": \"open\", \"minutes\": \"12\"}, {\"status\": \"closed\", \"minutes\": \"8\"}],\n    [{\"status\": \"pending\", \"minutes\": \"0\"}, {\"status\": \"open\", \"minutes\": \"5\"}],\n]\nfor rows in cases:                      # characterization check before any behaviour change\n    assert summary(rows) == legacy_summary(rows), rows\n    print(summary(rows))\n\n# Expected output:\n# {'count': 0, 'minutes': 0}\n# {'count': 1, 'minutes': 12}\n# {'count': 2, 'minutes': 5}",
            "language": "python"
          }
        ],
