        "id": "written-channel",
        "title": "18. Choose a channel that fits the issue",
        "takeaway": "A channel affects how easily people can clarify meaning, prepare and keep a record.",
        "stage": "advanced",
        "sections": [
          {
            "title": "Understand the distinction",
            "paragraphs": [
              "A channel affects how easily people can clarify meaning, prepare and keep a record. Text can be useful for facts and confirmation; a scheduled conversation can help explore a complex disagreement. Preferences and accessibility matter, so ask what works. Do not assume an in-person discussion is always superior or that a short message has a hostile tone."
            ]
          },
          {
            "title": "Mechanism and practical trade-off",
            "paragraphs": [
              "Written messages preserve a record, but delayed replies can make tone or meaning slower to clarify. Live conversations allow questions but may be inaccessible or difficult to schedule. A combined approach can work: exchange facts in writing, discuss the unresolved point in an agreed format, then record decisions. Ask about access needs instead of assuming one format suits everyone."
            ]
          },
          {
            "title": "Work through an everyday case",
            "paragraphs": [
              "A telephone discussion about a repair ends with two different understandings of the agreed cost."
            ],
            "example": "“I heard a limit of $80 including materials. Could you confirm the scope and amount in writing before work starts?” A written record suits this factual confirmation. If scope remains disputed, they can arrange an accessible discussion and record the resulting decision afterward.",
            "exampleFormat": "prose"
          }
        ],
        "exercise": {
          "prompt": "A group chat disagreement grows as people answer different parts of a long thread. Choose a next channel and write a neutral transition.",
          "solution": "We seem to be discussing two questions at once: timing and workload. Could we use a shared outline and a ten-minute call, or written comments if that works better? Afterwards we can record the agreed tasks in one message.",
          "solutionFormat": "prose",
          "checks": [
            "Channel choice considers access and clarification.",
            "The final decision will have an agreed record.",
            "Compare your answer with the example. Explain any different choice."
          ]
        },
        "quiz": {
          "question": "What should influence channel choice?",
          "options": [
            "Complexity, accessibility, preference and need for a record",
            "The belief that text always communicates hostility",
            "Who can pressure everyone into a call fastest"
          ],
          "correct": 0,
          "explanation": "Choose a channel using complexity, access needs, preference and the need for a record. No single format suits every conversation."
        },
