        "id": "reconciliation-capstone",
        "title": "12. Build an order-to-payment reconciliation",
        "takeaway": "A trustworthy report preserves populations, reconciles totals and exposes exceptions.",
        "sections": [
          {
            "title": "Concept",
            "paragraphs": [
              "Your stakeholder wants to know which orders are paid. Define the grain as one row per order, the scope as all five fixture orders, and the matching rule as `OrderId`. All amounts share one currency. Payment rows are staging observations: an unknown order is an exception to report, not evidence that an order should be invented.",
              "First aggregate the six payment rows by `OrderId`. Then left join the five orders to that result. Keep missing payment evidence separate from a known total of zero, and calculate outstanding as amount due minus matched payments. A negative outstanding amount means overpayment in this teaching contract.",
              "The report has two outputs: five order rows and an orphan-payment list. A grand-total subtraction of 390-285=105 is misleading for order balances because 20 of incoming payments belongs to unknown `OrderId` 999. Matched payments total 265, leaving net outstanding 125. Positive outstanding is 130; overpayment is 5; net 130-5=125."
            ],
            "example": ""
          },
          {
            "title": "Choose a status with CASE",
            "paragraphs": [
              "A searched `CASE` expression evaluates `WHEN` conditions in order and returns the result of the first true condition. `ELSE` supplies the fallback and `END` closes the expression. Put the missing-payment test first, then compare the matched payment total with the amount due. `COALESCE` can supply zero for balance arithmetic, but it would hide the distinction between no payment record and a recorded zero payment if you used it alone for status."
            ],
            "example": "SELECT OrderId,\n CASE WHEN Amount >= 100 THEN 'large' ELSE 'small' END AS Size\nFROM #LNOrders ORDER BY OrderId;\n-- 101 large; 102 small; 103 small; 104 large; 105 small"
          },
          {
            "title": "Example",
            "paragraphs": [
              "Expected order statuses: 101 paid, 102 paid, 103 underpaid by 10, 104 missing with 120 outstanding, 105 overpaid by 5. The orphan list contains 206/999/20. Check both row counts and values; a result containing the right total but duplicated rows is not acceptable."
            ],
            "example": ""
          },
          {
            "title": "Pitfalls",
            "paragraphs": [
              "A rerun must not append duplicate logical exceptions. A real pipeline needs source batch identifiers, source-row keys, duplicate policy, currency, effective time and approval rules. This capstone produces read-only results, so repeated execution does not post transactions. Duplicated `PaymentId` rows would be rejected by the fixture primary key; duplicate business events with different identifiers still require a separate policy."
            ],
            "example": ""
          }
        ],
        "exercise": {
          "prompt": "Write the two report outputs without looking at the solution. Explain why the orphan 20 is excluded from matched paid totals. Then describe how a duplicate payment event would change your design.",
          "starter": "-- 1 Aggregate payments by OrderId.\n-- 2 LEFT JOIN to the order population.\n-- 3 CASE classify missing/paid/underpaid/overpaid.\n-- 4 NOT EXISTS for orphan payments.",
          "solution": ";WITH Paid AS (\n SELECT OrderId,COUNT(*) AS PaymentCount,SUM(Amount) AS PaidAmount\n FROM #LNPayments GROUP BY OrderId\n)\nSELECT o.OrderId,o.Amount AS Due,COALESCE(p.PaidAmount,0) AS Paid,\n o.Amount-COALESCE(p.PaidAmount,0) AS Outstanding,\n CASE WHEN p.OrderId IS NULL THEN 'missing'\n      WHEN p.PaidAmount=o.Amount THEN 'paid'\n      WHEN p.PaidAmount<o.Amount THEN 'underpaid'\n      ELSE 'overpaid' END AS Status\nFROM #LNOrders o LEFT JOIN Paid p ON p.OrderId=o.OrderId\nORDER BY o.OrderId;\nSELECT p.PaymentId,p.OrderId,p.Amount FROM #LNPayments p\nWHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM #LNOrders o WHERE o.OrderId=p.OrderId)\nORDER BY p.PaymentId;",
          "checks": [
            "Exactly 5 order rows and 1 orphan row.",
            "Due 390, matched paid 265, net outstanding 125.",
            "Status counts: paid 2, underpaid 1, missing 1, overpaid 1.",
            "All payment amounts 285 = matched 265 + orphan 20.",
            "A duplicate-event policy is described without silently using DISTINCT on monetary values."
          ]
        },
        "quiz": {
          "question": "Why is 390 minus 285 not the correct net outstanding for known orders?",
          "options": [
            "The 285 includes 20 assigned to an unknown order",
            "SQL cannot subtract decimals",
            "All payment rows should be removed"
          ],
          "correct": 0,
          "explanation": "Only 265 is matched to known orders. Preserve the remaining 20 as an orphan exception instead of using it to reduce known balances."
        },
        "stage": "foundation",
