        "id": "edge-and-object-storage",
        "title": "11. Serve files from object storage and the edge",
        "stage": "intermediate",
        "takeaway": "Keep large files out of the database and application servers: store them as objects, let clients transfer them directly with short-lived signed URLs, and cache public ones at a CDN.",
        "sections": [
          {
            "title": "Concept",
            "paragraphs": [
              "Object storage keeps files as objects in buckets, addressed by key, with very high durability and practically unlimited capacity. It is cheaper than block storage or database rows for large binary data, but it is not a file system: there are no in-place edits or renames, and listing is slow at scale. Store the object key and metadata (owner, size, content type, checksum) in the database, and the bytes in the bucket.",
              "Routing every upload and download through application servers wastes their bandwidth and memory. Instead the server checks authorisation, then issues a presigned URL: a URL for one object and one method, signed with the server's credentials and valid for a few minutes. The client transfers the file directly with the storage service, which checks the signature and expiry.",
              "A content delivery network (CDN) caches responses at edge locations near users (lesson 10's caching rules apply). Public, versioned assets such as images and scripts suit long CDN lifetimes; private files should use short-lived signed URLs or signed cookies and must not be cached for other users."
            ]
          },
          {
            "title": "Design",
            "paragraphs": [
              "The sketch signs a download URL with an HMAC over the method, key and expiry, the way storage services do in more elaborate form. A request within the expiry with the correct signature is accepted; changing the key or arriving after expiry is rejected. Real services also bind headers and the credential scope, so use their SDKs rather than writing your own signer."
            ],
            "example": "import hashlib, hmac\n\nSECRET = b'server-side signing key'          # never sent to the client\n\ndef sign(method, key, expires):\n    message = f'{method}\\n{key}\\n{expires}'.encode()\n    return hmac.new(SECRET, message, hashlib.sha256).hexdigest()[:16]\n\ndef check(method, key, expires, signature, now):\n    if now > expires:\n        return 'rejected: expired'\n    if not hmac.compare_digest(signature, sign(method, key, expires)):\n        return 'rejected: bad signature'\n    return 'allowed'\n\nexpires = 1_000_000 + 300                     # five minutes after issue\nurl_sig = sign('GET', 'recordings/ws-42/session-1.mp4', expires)\nprint(check('GET', 'recordings/ws-42/session-1.mp4', expires, url_sig, now=1_000_120))\nprint(check('GET', 'recordings/ws-43/session-1.mp4', expires, url_sig, now=1_000_120))\nprint(check('GET', 'recordings/ws-42/session-1.mp4', expires, url_sig, now=1_000_301))\n\n# Expected output:\n# allowed\n# rejected: bad signature\n# rejected: expired"
          },
          {
            "title": "Tradeoffs",
            "paragraphs": [
              "A presigned URL is a bearer token: anyone holding it can use it until it expires, so keep lifetimes short, never log full URLs, and issue one per authorised request. Revoking access means waiting for expiry or rotating the signing key.",
              "Direct uploads arrive without passing through your code. Record the upload as pending, then confirm it with a storage event or a follow-up call that checks size, type and checksum before the file becomes visible; scan untrusted files before serving them. Lifecycle rules can move old objects to cheaper storage classes and delete abandoned uploads."
            ]
          }
        ],
        "exercise": {
          "prompt": "Workshop hosts upload recordings of up to 4 GB that only registered attendees may watch. Design the upload and playback flow, naming what the database stores, what the bucket stores and what the CDN may cache.",
          "solution": "Upload: the host asks the API for an upload; the API checks the host owns the workshop, creates a pending recording row (id, workshop, object key, expected size) and returns a presigned multipart upload URL. The client uploads directly to the bucket. A storage event or completion call verifies size, content type and checksum, then marks the row ready. Playback: the API checks the viewer is registered and returns a short-lived signed URL (or signed cookie) for the object or its CDN path. The database stores metadata and ownership; the bucket stores the bytes; the CDN may cache the video segments only behind signed access, never as public content, and the thumbnails can be public if they reveal nothing private.",
          "solutionFormat": "prose",
          "checks": [
            "Authorises before issuing any signed URL.",
            "Keeps bytes in the bucket and metadata in the database.",
            "Verifies uploads before marking them ready.",
            "Restricts CDN caching of private content to signed access."
          ]
        },
