        "id": "consistent-hashing",
        "title": "16. Rebalance partitions with consistent hashing",
        "stage": "intermediate",
        "takeaway": "Hashing modulo the node count moves most keys when a node is added; a hash ring with virtual nodes moves only about 1/N of them.",
        "sections": [
          {
            "title": "Concept",
            "paragraphs": [
              "Lesson 15 chose a partition key. The next question is how keys map to nodes. `hash(key) % N` spreads keys evenly, but changing N changes almost every result: adding a fifth node to four moves about 80% of keys, which for a cache means a burst of misses and for a database a huge migration.",
              "Consistent hashing places nodes at points on a ring of hash values. Each key belongs to the first node clockwise from its hash. Adding a node takes over only the keys between it and its predecessor, about 1/N of the total, and removing one hands its keys to its neighbour.",
              "With only one point per node, the gaps on the ring are uneven and some nodes get far more keys. Virtual nodes give each physical node many points, which evens out the share and lets a larger machine take more points. Rendezvous (highest random weight) hashing achieves the same minimal movement without a ring."
            ]
          },
          {
            "title": "Design",
            "paragraphs": [
              "Ten thousand learner keys are assigned to four nodes and then five. With modulo hashing about 80% move; with a ring of 100 virtual nodes per server about a fifth move, close to the ideal 1/5, and the five nodes hold similar shares."
            ],
            "example": "import bisect, hashlib\n\ndef h(text):\n    return int(hashlib.sha256(text.encode()).hexdigest()[:8], 16)\n\nkeys = [f'learner-{i}' for i in range(10_000)]\n\ndef modulo(nodes):\n    return {k: nodes[h(k) % len(nodes)] for k in keys}\n\ndef ring(nodes, vnodes=100):\n    points = sorted((h(f'{n}#{v}'), n) for n in nodes for v in range(vnodes))\n    hashes = [p for p, _ in points]\n    return {k: points[bisect.bisect(hashes, h(k)) % len(points)][1] for k in keys}\n\ndef moved(before, after):\n    return sum(before[k] != after[k] for k in keys) / len(keys)\n\nfour, five = ['a', 'b', 'c', 'd'], ['a', 'b', 'c', 'd', 'e']\nprint(f'modulo:           {moved(modulo(four), modulo(five)):.1%} of keys move')\nprint(f'consistent (100): {moved(ring(four), ring(five)):.1%} of keys move')\ncounts = {n: 0 for n in five}\nfor node in ring(five).values():\n    counts[node] += 1\nprint('keys per node:', counts)\n\n# Expected output:\n# modulo:           79.7% of keys move\n# consistent (100): 17.4% of keys move\n# keys per node: {'a': 2140, 'b': 1758, 'c': 2174, 'd': 2185, 'e': 1743}"
          },
          {
            "title": "Tradeoffs",
            "paragraphs": [
              "Consistent hashing reduces how much data moves; it does not move it. The system still needs to copy the affected keys, route reads correctly while they are in transit, and handle replicas, usually by placing copies on the next distinct nodes clockwise. A hot key (lesson 15) stays hot on whichever node owns it.",
              "Many databases instead split data into a fixed, large number of logical partitions and assign those to nodes through a directory. Rebalancing then moves whole partitions, which is easier to observe and throttle. Choose the managed behaviour of your datastore before building your own ring."
            ]
          }
        ],
