        "id": "search-systems",
        "title": "22. Keep a search index in step with the source of truth",
        "stage": "advanced",
        "takeaway": "A search engine answers text queries from an inverted index built from your data; it is a derived copy that lags the database and must be rebuildable.",
        "sections": [
          {
            "title": "Concept",
            "paragraphs": [
              "`LIKE '%join%'` cannot use an ordinary index (lesson 8) and scans every row. A search engine instead builds an inverted index: for each term, the list of documents containing it. A query looks up each term and combines the lists, then ranks results by relevance, for example with BM25, which rewards rare terms and frequent matches in short documents.",
              "Analysis decides what counts as a term: lowercasing, splitting on punctuation, removing very common words and stemming so that `join`, `joins` and `joined` match. The same analysis must apply to documents and queries. Search engines also provide filters, facets, typo tolerance and highlighting, which are hard to build in a relational database."
            ]
          },
          {
            "title": "Design",
            "paragraphs": [
              "The tiny index lowercases and splits text, so `Rows` finds lesson 2, and both terms of `tables joins` must match. `join` finds only lesson 1 and misses lesson 3's `joins`, because there is no stemming: a real analyser would reduce both to one term."
            ],
            "example": "import re\nfrom collections import defaultdict\n\nlessons = {\n    1: 'Join tables without losing records',\n    2: 'Window functions rank rows and running totals',\n    3: 'Indexes speed up joins on large tables',\n}\n\ndef tokens(text):\n    return re.findall(r'[a-z0-9]+', text.lower())\n\nindex = defaultdict(set)              # term -> ids of documents containing it\nfor doc_id, text in lessons.items():\n    for term in tokens(text):\n        index[term].add(doc_id)\n\ndef search(query):\n    sets = [index.get(term, set()) for term in tokens(query)]\n    return sorted(set.intersection(*sets)) if sets else []\n\nprint(dict(sorted((t, sorted(ids)) for t, ids in index.items() if t in ('joins', 'tables', 'rows'))))\nprint(search('tables joins'), search('join'), search('Rows'))\n\n# Expected output:\n# {'joins': [3], 'rows': [2], 'tables': [1, 3]}\n# [3] [1] [2]"
          },
          {
            "title": "Keeping it consistent",
            "paragraphs": [
              "The database stays the source of truth; the search index is a projection. Update it from change events, ideally written with the outbox pattern (lesson 21) or captured from the database log, so no committed change is missed. Updates are applied asynchronously, so search results can briefly show old data: re-check permissions and current state against the database when a user opens a result.",
              "Make index updates idempotent and ordered by a version number, so a delayed old event cannot overwrite a newer document. Keep a way to rebuild the whole index from the database into a new index and switch an alias to it; schema and analyser changes need exactly that, and it is also the recovery plan if the index is lost."
            ]
          }
        ],
        "exercise": {
          "prompt": "Workshop titles and descriptions must be searchable within a few seconds of being edited, and private workshops must never appear to other users. Design the indexing pipeline and the query path.",
          "solution": "Writes: the API updates the workshop in the database and, in the same transaction, inserts an outbox event with the workshop id and version. A relay publishes events; an indexer reads the current row and upserts the document if its version is newer than the indexed one, or deletes it. Documents include visibility and owner/tenant fields. Queries: the search service always filters by what the user may see (public, or their tenant), then the API re-checks each returned id against the database before showing details, which also hides results whose visibility changed since indexing. A full rebuild job writes a new index and switches an alias. Monitor lag between the database version and the indexed version.",
          "solutionFormat": "prose",
          "checks": [
            "Uses an outbox or log-based change capture so committed changes are not missed.",
            "Orders updates by version and handles deletes.",
            "Filters by permissions in the query and re-checks against the database.",
            "Provides a full rebuild path and monitors lag."
          ]
        },
